@Dasha Andriyenko Is it possible to have all highlight controls on the doorhanger by default, just in disabled state (grayed out)? Besides affects on assistive technology, it would also help users with cognitive difficulties and any other users to learn what the tool could provide. 

I personally think it's cool to know before you start the selection, that you then can just toggle selections off, for instance, if you're going to print the doc out without any marks, but still have some thoughts while initially reviewing it.

This won't be a showstopper though.

@Dasha Andriyenko @Shannon French 
1. Great!
2. Role: "Textarea" (probably); Properties: "placeholder: [Use on-screen string]"; Comments: "when 'Mark as decorative' radio button is checked, this text input becomes 'disabled' visually and programmatically - in this case it should not be focusable"
3. {Cancel, button, no props}
4. {Save, button, props: "is 'disabled' if the first radio button is checked and the textarea is empty"}

The second radio button "Mark as decorative" should not be separately focusable. It's expected behavior for keyboard users to 'Tab' to the radio button group (first radio or checked radio button would get focus) and then press arrow keys to move between radios - this is built into the radio button elements, but we want to mention that the second radio button is not in the focus order. Sometimes it's marked in designs with light circles and a note. Example: https://www.figma.com/file/i0SNijb8Twcn7czbzcDLYj/How-to-upsell-5-mask-limit?type=design&node-id=697-6937&mode=design&t=SlirT9PrCxDHcTYV-4 - it's not a radio button (items 3-6) but list items with the same keyboard navigation pattern)

Also, it's important that the focus order follows visual reading order (left-to-right for English and LTR langs), so the focus should move to "Cancel" button first, otherwise it would be confusing to sighted users and may be felt as jumping of the focus

@Dasha Andriyenko
1. Name: Just "Selected" (because the "image" would come from the role); Role: "Image"; Props: - [the comment could be used as "Comments:" item, maybe?)
